WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.240
I'm a professor
involved with EECS.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:13.170
And I [INAUDIBLE] 6.UAR I have
been a professor for 16 years

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:20.070
at MIT and taught 6.UAR for
the last two years or less--

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.120
maybe 18 months.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:27.570
So I'm assuming you guys are
junior and senior students,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.830
correct?

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:34.740
Actually, maybe you are
junior and sophomore, correct?

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.680
So we are here to tell you a
bit about the SuperUROP class.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.790
So before I tell
you about SuperUROP,

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.640
I actually want
to hear from you.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.870
What is SuperUROP?

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.430
What do you guys
know about SuperUROP?

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:48.930
Anyone?

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.463
Come on.

00:00:53.463 --> 00:00:55.780
It's like a year-long
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.680
It's year-long.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.210
What else?

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.730
I mean, guys, you are
here for some reason,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.550
other than the pizza.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.090
We understand pizza is
very important, but I mean,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.310
you have to know something.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:07.810
Come on.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.970
You have to do a [INAUDIBLE]
presentations [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.530
Yeah, so during
the class, you have

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.460
to present a poster at some
point, and also write a paper.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.650
What else?

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.880
Why are you
interested, actually?

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:23.380
Yeah.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.767
You got to continue
doing more research?

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:26.600
Oh, you do research.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.190
That's cool, yeah.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.570
OK, what else?

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.220
Why did you guys
come to this session,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.910
other than the free pizza?

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.150
We're supposed to get
paid, like, $25 an hour?

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.410
Oh, payments, right.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:40.910
Important.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.460
To get paid for research.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.500
Where else do you get
the class that pays you?

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.130
What else?

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.402
OK.

00:01:52.402 --> 00:01:53.110
Are you just shy?

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.095
MIT students are not shy.

00:01:56.095 --> 00:01:57.720
Satisfy the communication
requirements.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.450
It satisfies the
communication requirements.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.075
That's correct.

00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:08.485
Anything else you guys
know about the class?

00:02:08.485 --> 00:02:09.440
Yes?

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.390
I want to learn.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:12.820
OK, great.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:13.690
Know nothing.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:14.470
Wants to learn.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:18.300
OK, so let me tell
you about the class.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.150
So SuperUROP class
has two components.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.920
One of them is research.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.030
So you can think of
this as two pillars.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.540
To some extent, they
are not very connected

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.370
as much as other
classes where you

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.078
have components of other classes
that are very mixed together.

00:02:37.078 --> 00:02:38.620
So in a sense, that
is one component,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.040
which is your research.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.970
So if you guys
decide to join 6.UAR,

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:47.290
then each one of you will
be associated with a faculty

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.170
professor and a mentor--

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.690
maybe a graduate
student or postdoc--

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.900
and you will be working
on a research project

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.440
within that group of
people, in their lab.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.890
And that would be
the research section

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.320
of this SuperUROP class.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.180
And then, in addition
to do research,

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.690
that is the class itself.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.650
There are the lectures
and the recitations.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.070
And those are more focused on
how you present your research.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.680
So you are doing the research
in the lab with your professor

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.200
and mentor, and then
you go to the class,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.600
you hear from professors
about their research.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.240
So every lecture,
there are people,

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.310
like professors and
people from the industry,

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.410
that come and tell you
about their research.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.300
And then, in the recitation
and some of the lectures,

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.600
we also talk about how you
can present to all research,

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.050
whether that presentation
is through posters

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.990
or through academic
papers that you write.

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.120
And hopefully, by the
end, you can submit

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.620
to a workshop or conference.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.470
So these are the two components.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.630
Now, of course, it is also
different from other classes

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:56.620
at MIT in the sense that
it is a two semester class.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.230
So each semester,
you get six units.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.300
So in the fall semester,
you get the first six unit,

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.110
and then you complete the
six other units in the spring

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:11.260
for total of 12 units of class.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.800
Now, those 12 units--

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.820
so people, when
they hear six units,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.115
they expect very
lightweight class.

00:04:19.115 --> 00:04:21.490
But actually, the one thing
that you have to keep in mind

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.800
is that the six units
of class are actually

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.280
for the class-- are for the
time you spend in the lectures

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.620
and on the assignments
in the class.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.080
And that is the workload.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.680
It's six units of class.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.040
But then, you are expected
to spend 10 hours per week

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.270
on your research.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.160
And that's what
you are paid for.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.040
That's where the
money comes from.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.540
OK?

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.890
So keep in mind
these two things.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:50.410
So research-- 10 hours per week
doing your research project

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.300
with your professor and mentor.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.090
And then, in class,
you are planning

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.907
how to guide and
present your research,

00:04:57.907 --> 00:04:59.740
whether in the fall
semester-- typically, we

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.670
work on the poster.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.890
In the spring, we
work on a paper.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.810
And that has its own set
of assignment associated.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.610
Those are six units, but the
money is for the research,

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.770
and together, that is a class.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:12.270
OK?

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.750
Now let me tell you a bit
about the research part.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:22.170
So as I said, each one of you
would be working with someone,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.660
like a professor at MIT.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.672
Many of you probably
are EECS students,

00:05:27.672 --> 00:05:29.880
and perhaps you are going
to work with some professor

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.080
here, in EECS.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.410
Actually, who among
you is not in EECS?

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.190
Can you raise your hand?

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:37.350
OK, yeah.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.830
So we have, in the
class, some people

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.700
from another part of
the engineering school.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.020
And for you guys,
you also are going

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.020
to have a mentor and
professor that you are working

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.310
with in your department.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.790
And these are some
example projects.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.010
So here, you see some people
working on nanomaterials,

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:00.200
some people working on
circuits, some people working

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.130
on robots, many people working
on AI problems and machine

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.330
learning.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.560
It's your favorite project.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.350
You get to pick, and you
spend the semester on it.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:14.750
And at the end of each semester,
you present your research.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.680
So there is a showcase
where you prepare a poster,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.230
and you stand there,
and we invite people

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.260
from the industry, we
invite the faculty, and also

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:25.550
your fellow students.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.145
And everyone is there,
mingling, and we have food,

00:06:28.145 --> 00:06:29.840
and it's a very nice event.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.260
But also, you get to tell
people about your research,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.750
based on what you learned
throughout the semester.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.780
So that is one showcase at the
end of the fall, one showcase

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.250
at the end of this spring.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:48.550
Now, as I mentioned, the
research part has up to $3,000

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.670
if you spent 10 hours
per week in the lab.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:57.140
And that's $3,000 per
semester, so total of $6,000.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.720
And in addition to that,
basically, as I said,

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.850
the 10 hours on the
research, that is not like--

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.340
you can't count the time
in the class and the time

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.050
on assignment for the class.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:13.250
That is just an
hour for research.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.440
Now, for students who are from--

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.940
so there are many departments
in the engineering school,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.310
and my guess of the people
who raised their hand

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.140
and said they are
not part of EECS,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.830
as many of those departments
are already participating

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.510
departments in the class.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.780
But if your department is not a
participating department, which

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:35.810
means that it did
not donate money

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:39.650
to support their students,
then you can actually either

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:43.040
talk to your department
about the support,

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.500
or you can take the
class just as if it's

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.970
like a more formal UROP class.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.860
So you can get those
credits, the academic unit.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.640
But then, if you are not from
the supporting departments,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.680
then, basically, we cannot
pay you in the class.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.800
OK, so what are
the deliverables?

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.560
So in the fall
term, as I said-- so

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.060
the deliverable is the class.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.170
First, attendance is mandatory.

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:09.850
I'm sorry.

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:17.380
But it's like we invite faculty
and professors here in EECS.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.450
We invite people
from the industry.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:23.020
So it is very important that
you actually show up to classes.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.140
And during the class,
we ask a question

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.790
about the lecture, about
whatever those people have

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.610
presented, by the
end of the class.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.690
And you enter the
answer online, and that

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.340
will be your attendance.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:38.870
So in addition to classes--

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.179
the class is one hour per week--

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:46.160
and that is also one hour of
recitation every other week.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.790
And there is about 15
minutes every other week

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:54.050
just checking in with
your TA, making sure

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:56.720
that everything is going right,
and if you have problems,

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.370
you can talk to your TAs.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.340
They can give you, also,
feedback about your research,

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.590
in general.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:07.120
And at the end of the fall
term, you prepare a poster,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.650
and then you
present that poster.

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:14.810
Now, for the spring, it's
very, very similar to the fall,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.300
except that instead
of for poster,

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.860
now you are working
on a 10 page paper

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.900
that you finish by the end
of the spring semester.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.050
And you still go and
update your poster,

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.760
because now you
have more results,

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.980
and you present
your poster again

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:30.510
at the end of the
spring semester,

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.930
but now was full fledged
results and understanding

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:35.030
of your problem.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:37.850
And many people ask
the question, OK,

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:42.490
so what is a grade that I get
by the end of the fall term?

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.490
But that's not the right
way to think about it.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.320
You don't really get to grade
at the end of the fall term.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.290
So it is a two term class.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.150
So think about at the
end of the fall term.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.220
It's like you're
finishing your midterm.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:58.330
So you can know whether you
did well or not as well,

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:02.110
but you don't really have
a grade at that point.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:05.680
It is one class that extends
across the fall and the spring,

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.110
and you're really get
your grade, whatever that

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:09.750
is, at the end of this spring.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.046
OK?

00:10:15.046 --> 00:10:18.960
You don't get any units if
you don't finish [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:19.900
Yeah.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.050
So it's one class.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.170
So again, think about it exactly
as at the end of the fall,

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:26.060
it's kind of like you
finish your midterm.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:27.560
If you drop the
class at that point,

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:31.490
you don't get any
units from the class.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:35.590
So also, there was a
particular opportunity

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.950
from a particular donor who
saw that MIT students  don't get

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.930
interested enough in
humanities, and he

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:43.960
wanted to promote the
connection between humanities

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.100
and engineering.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:47.890
So he donated for
people who are to pay

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:50.650
for projects that combine
engineering with humanities.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.450
So if you are
interested in that,

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:53.950
you can apply for
those projects.

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:02.540
OK, so also, you
get a certificate.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.090
So you get a nice
piece of paper.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.930
It's a certificate, and you
pass your SuperUROP class,

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:11.375
and we have a celebration.

00:11:11.375 --> 00:11:15.927
I'll bring cookies and
food and all that stuff.

00:11:15.927 --> 00:11:16.635
We take pictures.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:25.550
OK, so I'm going to
stop a bit here and ask

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.520
whether you have any questions.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:28.020
Yes?

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.400
How does [INAUDIBLE] work?

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:37.610
OK, so online, there is a site
that has proposals or project

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:41.600
descriptions by professors
in various departments

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.850
in the School of Engineering.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.750
And you can go there and
look at those proposals,

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:50.180
talk to the PI, talk
to the professor,

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.470
and see whether it's
a good match for you.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:55.190
And then at the
end, you are asked,

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:58.070
based on understanding
of that project

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:02.300
and understanding what
you talked to the faculty,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.662
write a short proposal that--

00:12:04.662 --> 00:12:05.870
I'm going to do this project.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:09.290
This is my proposal for the
work that I'm going to do here.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.528
And I'm going to tell you
the exact deadline for that.

00:12:12.528 --> 00:12:22.390
[INAUDIBLE]

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:24.802
Yeah, that's possible.

00:12:24.802 --> 00:12:25.774
Yeah.

00:12:25.774 --> 00:12:26.746
Yes.

00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:31.120
Can you let us, if we have
an existing UROP [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.560
because they're
just [INAUDIBLE]??

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:37.280
Yeah, you can do it, but we
ask that the work that you do

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.950
for SuperUROP is new research.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:44.390
So you can extend your project
and do the next step with it,

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:45.860
and that would be
your SuperUROP,

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:51.510
but we don't accept that-- oh,
I did this UROP before, and here

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:52.310
is my paper.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:53.810
Here's all the work that I did.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.480
Now, pay me $6,000.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.633
That doesn't work.

00:12:58.633 --> 00:12:59.300
Other questions?

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:00.660
Yes?

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.080
For this, is it that you
apply to this program

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:07.280
and then you look for a
project to work on within it?

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:10.310
Or you apply for a project?

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:12.710
To make things a bit
easier for you guys,

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:17.690
we ask the professors to
upload projects on the site.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:20.060
And you can go and
look at those projects

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:23.120
and see whether you are
interested in any of them,

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.060
talk to the professor, and see
whether you have a good match.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:31.050
But also, as your friend
here said, basically,

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:32.870
if you have already
a project and you

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:36.590
have a faculty who is
interested in mentoring you

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:40.410
as you are doing this
project, that's fine, as well.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:40.910
Yeah?

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:43.650
Does your credit have to have an
intersection with [INAUDIBLE]??

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.790
It has to have intersection
with engineering,

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:50.460
but this is under the
School of Engineering.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.340
There are-- I think most of
the departments in the School

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.620
of the engineering are
supporting departments,

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:58.270
but it doesn't have
to be course 6.0.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:03.090
So as long as it has an
engineering component.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:05.300
So if it's an [INAUDIBLE]?

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:10.810
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:11.310
Yeah.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:14.835
Yeah, of course it's possible.

00:14:14.835 --> 00:14:17.805
So you said [INAUDIBLE]
proposals for our project

00:14:17.805 --> 00:14:19.290
after the deadline.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.755
[INAUDIBLE] if you want to
work on the same project,

00:14:22.755 --> 00:14:24.735
[INAUDIBLE] research
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:33.450
Yeah, it is OK.

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:36.630
But keep in mind
that, basically, you

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:38.010
have to take responsibility.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.470
So if you change things
later in the term,

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.890
or after some deadlines,
we are flexible,

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.230
but it means that
you have to ramp up

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:47.740
in a shorter period of time.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:49.650
So as long as you
are responsible

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:53.720
and you know that you
can do that, no problem.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:56.250
OK, so I'll take more questions.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:58.130
But let me just ask you guys--

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.190
so why should you do 6.UAR?

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:03.766
Why should you do SuperUROP?

00:15:03.766 --> 00:15:04.650
Hm?

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:07.440
There are many classes at MIT.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:09.330
Many of them that
are very interesting.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:10.897
Why should you do SuperUROP?

00:15:10.897 --> 00:15:12.210
Grad school.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:13.220
Grad school.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:16.662
OK, why SuperUROP could
be useful for grad school?

00:15:16.662 --> 00:15:21.590
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.530
Yeah, I mean basically,
research is essential

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:25.970
if you want to go
to grad school.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.100
In fact, I mean, at
this stage, people

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.920
expect that you have some
publication or at least

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.730
some submission.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:34.970
So to go to--

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:36.570
again don't get scare---

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.830
I'm just seeing like,
the faces are flashing.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:43.100
But I mean, basically, the
more research background

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:46.640
you have as you apply for grad
school, the better position

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.810
you are in to get accepted
and to get the interest,

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:54.920
if it is a good faculty
in that department.

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:55.520
Why else?

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:00.220
Hm?

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:01.900
Why should you do this?

00:16:05.796 --> 00:16:07.260
Yes?

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.870
Invent.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:10.750
This is like--

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:12.210
I was an MIT student, actually.

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.600
I didn't do my undergrad here.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.380
I did my grad school here.

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:19.670
And I remember, I when I
was a graduate student,

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:23.630
I mean, basically, MIT students
would not stop talking.

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:26.870
Just ask questions that
are, in many cases,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.010
even random questions.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:30.230
Just keep talking.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:31.890
Now, you guys are so shy.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.740
Just like-- a question.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.120
Nobody asking any questions.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:36.040
Yes?

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:39.560
I think it's a chance
to pursue [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.260
A chance to pursue--

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:45.350
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:48.470
Yeah, so actually, honestly,
the most important reason

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:53.150
to do this is to try actual
research, particularly if you

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:56.060
are passionate about something--
like some particular field

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:59.610
of research-- to try to
get into understanding it.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:03.363
So just to give you an
example, so you guys--

00:17:03.363 --> 00:17:04.280
I mean, I don't know--

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:09.569
how many of you have been
engaged in research so far?

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:10.800
OK, so that's good.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:13.260
Like a reasonable
percentage of you.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:15.569
But if you think
about it, I mean, MIT,

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:17.369
what is so special about MIT?

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.410
I mean, the classes are
great, but really, this

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:20.800
is a research university.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:24.150
This is the place where the
best and most interesting

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.700
research in engineering happens.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:30.720
And if you are spending four
years here and graduating

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.780
with a degree, whether you
are going to grad school,

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:37.080
or going to the industry, or
starting your own company--

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:40.770
whatever it is-- if you
haven't done research

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:45.450
and haven't experienced it,
then you missed the whole point.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.902
You missed the best thing here.

00:17:47.902 --> 00:17:49.110
Yeah, you've got the classes.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:51.170
But that's really
not the whole story.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:53.300
That's a very, very
small part of the story.

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:57.950
It's like going to Paris and
not seeing the Eiffel Tower,

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:00.030
for example.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:03.530
It's like everyone's
going to ask you about it.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:07.990
So it is an opportunity to
get exposed to research.

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:10.510
Some people really
love it after that,

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:13.840
and they decide they want
to go to grad school,

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.970
and they want to
become researchers.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:18.890
Very few people discover,
no, this is not for me.

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:22.550
But at least you tried it.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:26.490
So the most important reason
is actually to do research.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:30.210
What else do you get
by taking this class?

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:30.980
Yes?

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.922
Half a question, half
make sure you can do it.

00:18:33.922 --> 00:18:35.505
it gives you more
experience with what

00:18:35.505 --> 00:18:37.080
you can get out of a UROP--

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.660
like a normal UROP--
perhaps you're

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.910
not guaranteed that you're
going to get experience out

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.877
of it in terms of posters,
submissions, writing a paper,

00:18:44.877 --> 00:18:47.210
whereas this would be a more
guaranteed and fast-tracked

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:48.640
route?

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:49.140
Exactly.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:53.010
So in a sense, for people
who did UROP and had

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:57.480
like a very structured and
organized UROP experience,

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:00.150
you got a lot of the
experience with research.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.200
But what the class
allows you to do

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.530
is to do it in a
more systematic way.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.870
And for people who haven't
done it, to actually do it.

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:10.680
But basically, because not
only are you doing research,

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:14.460
you are also presenting
that research as like--

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:15.950
OK, tell me about your research.

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:17.940
Prepare a poster
about your research.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:19.760
Talk to me about your research.

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:23.910
Then you are actually learning
as a whole process of doing

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.310
the research, while you
are doing that in the lab,

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.620
but also, how do you
present your research?

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:32.550
How do you eventually get a
technical paper, hopefully,

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:33.540
out of it?

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.810
And be an effective
researcher, both in terms

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.000
of doing the actual work
and communicating it

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.100
to other people.

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:43.517
And being paid for it.

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:48.170
OK, other reasons to do it?

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:58.630
How about the
communication requirements?

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.470
How many of you have this
communication requirement?

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:05.320
So you get done with it.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:09.130
So these are a few
reasons, right?

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:12.140
Always, in the feedback, people
are happy about the money.

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:15.970
But I don't think that's
the most important reason.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:19.360
The most important reason
is the research and being

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:20.600
exposed to the research.

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:23.260
That's the one thing
that you should not

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:25.450
miss after four years at MIT.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:31.470
And you get your
CI-M requirement.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.200
So other things about
the class that are cool

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:39.990
is, like, it's really even cool
for me as professor at MIT.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:43.690
So every lecture, we
invite one to two speakers.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.900
And those people
are MIT faculty.

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:49.290
So imagine, those people
are typically paid--

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:51.870
I don't know-- humongous
amounts of money

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.060
to come and give a
lecture somewhere.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:56.670
But they come and give you a--

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.980
20 of them come to
one of those class

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.470
to tell you about
their research,

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:02.460
to tell you about
what they are doing.

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:04.470
And it is really a
great opportunity

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:06.360
that doesn't happen otherwise.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:11.250
And you get to see research
across the whole broad spectrum

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:16.500
of engineering, from machine
learning, to computer vision,

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:20.640
to NLP, to circuits,
to computer systems,

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:26.280
to networks, to, sometimes,
people in material sciences,

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:30.650
in biology, or bioengineering,
digital health.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.850
It's a whole broad spectrum.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:36.240
And yeah, you are in
the lab doing one thing,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.090
focusing on your problem
and your project,

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:41.760
but it gives you
perspective, and that's

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.490
across all of these
fields and areas.

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:46.500
And that's really--

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.020
I mean, as you get
to experience it,

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:50.880
it actually is a
great experience,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.260
if you are interested.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:53.890
I mean, you are
all at MIT, you I

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:59.390
assume you are interested
in science and engineering.

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.950
OK so where to learn
more about the SuperUROP.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:06.520
So you can go to
superurop.mit.edu.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.820
And if you want to
apply, then you apply

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:16.038
at superurop-apply.mit.edu,
and this is the contact email.

00:22:16.038 --> 00:22:17.580
I mean, if you go
to the site, you'll

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:19.900
find all the email
and the information.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:22.155
But the other thing
that I want to end with

00:22:22.155 --> 00:22:25.530
is some of the deadlines,
so that all of you

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:26.700
are aware of them.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:31.890
So April 3-- so as we said,
basically, go on the website,

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:34.710
try to check for the
projects that are available.

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:37.740
And you are also free to try
to create your own project, as

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.700
long as you find
some professor who

00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:42.090
is willing to
supervise that project.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:46.920
So April 3 is deadline of having
intent and saying that you

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.530
want to join the SuperUROP.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:53.620
April 27 is the deadline
for submitting a proposal.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:57.000
So, as we said, you go and
talk to that professor,

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.470
you understand the
project better,

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:01.870
and then you submit
about one page

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:03.780
of description of that project.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:06.780
Typically, we ask you to
have some figures just

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:09.660
to show that you have some
understanding of the project

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.180
that you're going to work on.

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:16.320
And in June, we announce
the funding and who

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:18.060
is going to be in the class.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:19.750
Do you want to add something?

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:22.650
So that says include
that are recommendations

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.342
for primary supervisor.

00:23:26.342 --> 00:23:27.550
You don't have to include it.

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:32.790
You have to ask your supervisor
to upload it independently.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:34.710
Is that for recommendation,
or just that

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:37.440
they accept a student
to work with them.

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:42.360
They say, I'm enthusiastic
about supervising this student.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.290
I believe they will be
successful in the project they

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:46.380
have proposed.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:48.810
Yeah, so it's not like
they have to write

00:23:48.810 --> 00:23:51.713
a full letter of
recommendation, just

00:23:51.713 --> 00:23:53.130
that they are
saying that they are

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:57.430
enthusiastic to work with you
and they have talked to you.

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:01.300
So that's the last slide I have.

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:05.750
I can answer
two more questions.

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:06.250
Yes?

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:07.923
How does the
application review work?

00:24:07.923 --> 00:24:09.090
Who reviews the application?

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:11.720
What are the chances
of getting in?

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:15.470
So the chances are very high.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:19.100
Unless we believe that,
for example, your GPA--

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.740
if a person's GPA is very
low and we think that they

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:23.350
are taking too many classes.

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:24.888
Or the GPA might
be high, but they

00:24:24.888 --> 00:24:26.930
are taking too many classes,
they don't have time

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:31.180
to spend 10 hours
a week in the lab,

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:35.150
then those cases are the ones
that get rejected, mostly.

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:36.680
Most people get accepted.

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:40.140
So as long as you are
doing reasonably well,

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:41.790
you are not
overloading yourself,

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:45.200
we think that you have a
reasonable chance of success

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:48.290
succeeding in the class,
then you get accepted.

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:53.440
And the advisor has to, of
course, agree to supervise you.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:55.480
Who's reviewing
the applications?

00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:56.220
This staff.

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.220
The SuperUROP staff.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:04.030
So the staff is a collection
of faculty, research,

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:06.640
senior research staff, and TAs.

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:11.833
So if you were to
not have funding,

00:25:11.833 --> 00:25:14.250
and you were doing this for
credit, how would credit work?

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.870
Would it just be 10 hours
of credit, like a UROP,

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:19.790
and then it would be
graded or ungraded?

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:22.070
So it would be like UROp.

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:23.960
And Dorothy has been
with the class for--

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:27.530
I don't know-- since it
started in 2012, so she has--

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:29.490
I didn't hear the question.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:32.630
So if the SuperUROP
is for a credit,

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:35.080
would it be 10 hours of
graded, ungraded credit,

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:37.350
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:38.060
Pass/fail.

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:40.240
Pass/fail.

00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:41.260
Yes?

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:43.890
Should that transcript-- does
it need to be an official one,

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:47.690
or can you just take a
screenshot of the grade report?

00:25:47.690 --> 00:25:50.570
Can she take a screenshot
of the transcript?

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:53.610
Does it have to be an
official transcript?

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.820
So for people to submit
their transcript,

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:58.605
is it OK if they just take a
screenshot of the transcript.

00:25:58.605 --> 00:26:00.050
Yeah, unofficial.

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:02.030
Don't spend money.

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:03.050
Don't get it signed.

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:05.530
We can't read it.

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:07.260
There's digital [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.740
And we try to be flexible.

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:12.430
The objective is not to
make you go out of your way.

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:15.410
Is there a set
list of professors

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:16.970
that can be SuperUROP
supervisors,

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:21.350
or can you get any, as long
as you establish contact

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:22.610
with them?

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:27.050
So their department has to
be part of the participating

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:28.023
department.

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:33.890
For example,
mechanical engineering

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:35.480
is a participating
department, so you

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:37.910
can get a professor there.

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:39.870
I know there might be
some department x--

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:44.170
I know biology, probably, is
not a participating department.

00:26:44.170 --> 00:26:46.790
And you can't just have
somebody in biology.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:49.130
If you have somebody
in biology, then we

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:52.430
ask that you have someone
as kind of like a shadow

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:55.840
advisor in EECS.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.866
But you're in 67--

00:26:58.866 --> 00:27:01.331
yeah.

00:27:01.331 --> 00:27:04.790
they can definitely [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:07.100
And you guys can send emails.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:09.860
If you have specific questions
that are not answered,

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:13.110
or you have a specific
case, please send email

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:17.210
to this account.

00:27:17.210 --> 00:27:19.073
Highly likely to
get in the class,

00:27:19.073 --> 00:27:20.740
and then the next
question is whether we

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:23.170
have money to support it.

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:27.520
So that's a little bit
more challenging question.

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:31.150
What is the difference
between [INAUDIBLE] students

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.550
getting the advice [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:36.240
Getting into the class means
you can go to the class

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:41.040
and do your research for credit.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.440
You know, EECS works
hard to raise money

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:46.893
to support SuperUROP
students, and we

00:27:46.893 --> 00:27:48.560
don't know how many
we're going to have,

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:51.220
and we don't know whether the
sponsors are going to show up.

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:53.530
We're optimistic.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:56.760
So basically, the department
raises money every year

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.670
to pay the students.

00:27:58.670 --> 00:28:01.460
So we don't know ahead
of time how much money

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:03.790
we will be able to
raise, and therefore,

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:09.530
how many students, in total, we
are we can accept for funding.

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:12.620
You know, EECS students
working with EECS

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:16.150
professors, that's highly
likely to get funded.

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:20.690
EECS students working with
AeroAstro or MechE professors,

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:21.515
that's--

00:28:21.515 --> 00:28:24.630
we funded them last year,
but we have enough money.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:27.540
You're better off just working
with AeroAstro professors,

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:29.060
even like--

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:31.100
Well, it depends
whether AeroAstro

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:33.650
can find any [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:34.730
Maybe we can take it--

00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:39.190
I'm just worried about, like,
there are two more speakers.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.290
I can come to the end of the
class and answer your question

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:44.080
or Dorothy can
answer your question,

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:47.170
but I want to allow the other
two speakers enough time.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:56.000
Should I go next?

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.300
Either one of you guys.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:28:59.710
If I slide down this?

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:00.210
The slides?

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:20.863
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:29:20.863 --> 00:29:25.890
So the Quest for Intelligence
is an MIT-wide initiative

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:30.120
on the science, engineering,
and application of intelligence.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:33.750
And this year, we are
very happy to offer over

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:39.730
20 SuperUROP
possibilities of funding.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:43.520
And so I'm one of the
directors of the Quest,

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:48.210
and I'm going to give you a
brief overview of the topics.

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:51.750
But most of the opportunities
related to the Quest

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.550
are not yet on the website.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:57.810
We hope to have posted all of
them next week on the website,

00:29:57.810 --> 00:30:01.690
so you have kind of
a preview right now.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:06.760
So the projects come in
certain different types

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:08.620
of organization.

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:11.500
There is what we call
the bridge project.

00:30:11.500 --> 00:30:12.880
So the Quest Bridge

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:13.810
is a community

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:16.690
of research scientists
and software engineers

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:19.690
who we are working with
undergraduate and graduate

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:23.500
students to [INAUDIBLE]
very big and large project.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:26.140
And you have a few
titles over here.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:30.550
If you're interested to work
with our students on a topic

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:35.020
that is highlighted here,
from gaze tracking

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:38.590
in childhood, or
greenhouse gas impact--

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:42.040
very important--
face recognition,

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:45.730
anything related to a
biology, like some topics,

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:49.030
then this might be something
that is good for you.

00:30:49.030 --> 00:30:51.520
So we have a few on this.

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:55.900
There is a second type
of SuperUROP opportunity

00:30:55.900 --> 00:30:59.500
that's actually more tied to
brain and cognitive science

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:03.130
development, and we call
them the Quest Science.

00:31:03.130 --> 00:31:04.330
And they have a nickname--

00:31:04.330 --> 00:31:06.920
Scaling AI the Human Way.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:10.540
And this is an ambitious
research program that is lead

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:15.220
by Professor Josh Tenenbaum
and many of the faculty at MIT

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:20.420
CSAIL, and it's--
how can we build and grow

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:24.910
a system that will have some
intelligence resembling human

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:26.120
and child?

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:30.750
So there will be a few
projects, also, on that topic.

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:36.420
A further class of project, they
are on computational science

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.340
and engineering project--

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:40.890
those will be faculty driven.

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:44.280
So when you go next
week to the website

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:46.290
and you see some projects
you're interested in,

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:49.110
if you see Quest
written somewhere--

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:53.730
Quest for Intelligence-- it will
be one of those funded by us.

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:59.750
And here, you have three examples
that are on various topics.

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:05.460
And we also have a
project that are like--

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:09.420
we don't put an ad about
it, but I tell you about it.

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:12.860
Those will be any
faculty or project

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:19.190
you will propose to a
faculty related to fintech.

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:22.640
Then, we could pick it
up, if it's a project that

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:25.560
is interesting in that area.

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:28.300
So this is sort of this--

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:31.640
either you interested in this
larger topic, the finance

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:34.370
industry, and many
of the machine

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:36.860
learning algorithms that
are related to this, then

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:39.510
you can find the faculty
and come back to us saying,

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:42.890
hey, I found a faculty,
I have a good idea,

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:44.220
can we do something together?

00:32:44.220 --> 00:32:46.580
So those are more open.

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:50.730
And we also have some that are
related to the MIT-IBM Watson

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:51.260
AI lab.

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:55.200
I'm also the director of this
lab on a variety of topic.

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:58.070
You will see the variety
of topic next week.

00:32:58.070 --> 00:33:01.070
And what I would like to
emphasize today is certainly--

00:33:01.070 --> 00:33:07.130
for the bridge project, the
computational engineering one,

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:09.720
some of the fintech,
and the MIT-IBM--

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:12.710
if you are interested
to go to industry,

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:16.370
then those are, potentially,
sorts of project

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:21.260
where you will do research
at MIT with an MIT professor,

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:23.750
but more aligned to some
of the questions that are

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:25.520
interesting in the industry.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.320
So therefore,
beyond grad school,

00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:29.480
those could offer
you the possibility

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:34.400
to have also somebody from
IBM close-up advising you,

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:37.280
but also start touching
some of the questions

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:39.350
interesting for industry.

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:42.150
Again, most of them will
be online next week.

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:43.843
Thank you.

00:33:43.843 --> 00:33:45.260
Is there a question
I should take?

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:51.604
All right.

00:33:51.604 --> 00:33:54.044
Thank you.

