00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:37.772
So the reason that I
wanted to go into SuperUROP

00:37:37.772 --> 00:37:39.730
was because I was planning
to do an  MEng

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:42.370
and I thought that it would
be more structured and more

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:45.940
long-term than just
a semester long UROP.

00:37:45.940 --> 00:37:49.800
And so the main reason was to
prepare for the MEng

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:51.520
and then I also wanted
like the mentorship

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:53.770
in the lab and
also the classroom,

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:56.900
to improve the research
and communication skills.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:37:59.590
And so my project was working
with Professor Thomas Heldt,

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:03.250
and then I worked with a
doctor at Beth Israel Medical

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:05.320
Center named Wendy Timpson.

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.690
And so my project was on
leveraging clinical data

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:10.120
to optimize oxygen
delivery to preterm

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:14.030
infants in the neonatal
intensive care unit.

00:38:14.030 --> 00:38:16.000
And so we were just
analyzing different factors

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:18.932
that may be affecting the
oxygenation of preterm infants

00:38:18.932 --> 00:38:20.890
and then trying to figure
out if we can improve

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:23.560
the clinical workflow to
maximize the amount of time

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:26.200
patients spend in their
target oxygen range

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:29.890
to prevent mortality
and morbidity.

00:38:29.890 --> 00:38:31.390
And so, as I
mentioned before, it's

00:38:31.390 --> 00:38:32.900
a really guided
research experience.

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:34.900
I thought the weekly
meetings with the professor

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:36.765
and the clinician
was really helpful.

00:38:36.765 --> 00:38:38.140
And so, if you
guys do SuperUROP,

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:40.723
I would recommend like setting
that up because it really keeps

00:38:40.723 --> 00:38:43.210
the research moving along.

00:38:43.210 --> 00:38:45.130
I also got a bunch of
great opportunities,

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:48.040
such as shadowing the
neonatologist and a nurse

00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:50.560
at the hospital and
just learning more

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:52.330
about the hospital workflow.

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:54.880
And then there were
weekly lab meetings

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:58.240
where we would learn about other
researchers in the lab and what

00:38:58.240 --> 00:38:59.675
research they were doing.

00:38:59.675 --> 00:39:01.300
And then I also got
to present at that,

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:05.110
so I got feedback on my
work and different ideas

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:06.940
that I could incorporate.

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:09.940
I also got to present at the
New England Perinatal Society

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:13.510
meeting, which is like a mini
conference for neonatologist

00:39:13.510 --> 00:39:15.670
and clinicians in
the New England area.

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:18.070
And so that was a
really good experience

00:39:18.070 --> 00:39:21.610
and definitely helped with
presentation practice.

00:39:21.610 --> 00:39:24.400
And just overall, it was really
great to work on the project

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:28.000
and really dive deep into it
over the course of the year.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.680
On the classroom
side of things, I

00:39:29.680 --> 00:39:33.440
thought 6.UAR was really
interesting and useful.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:36.220
The weekly lectures
with the researchers

00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:37.440
presenting their work--

00:39:37.440 --> 00:39:41.330
it was really cool to see like
different domains across campus

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:44.740
and just stay up to
date on research at MIT.

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:48.160
And then there were also TAs
and communication instructors

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:50.560
that would provide helpful
feedback for the research

00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:52.428
paper and also the poster.

00:39:52.428 --> 00:39:56.100
You should tell them
about any downsides.

00:39:56.100 --> 00:39:58.680
I don't know if there
were any downsides.

00:39:58.680 --> 00:40:02.970
I know some people have said
that some of the assignments

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:06.690
might be like busy work.

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:09.930
But personally, I thought that
everything was really useful

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:14.100
and really helped guide
writing the paper.

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:18.160
So yeah, I don't
know about downsides.

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:21.110
If there were any, I wanted to
you to save them beforehand.

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:23.390
Yeah, off the top
of my head, I can't

00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:26.456
think of anything
negative about SuperUROP.

00:40:26.456 --> 00:40:29.910
This might not be for you,
just for anyone else to answer.

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:31.830
Is there ever a time--

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:35.940
because you're doing research,
sometimes research work out,

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:36.930
right?

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:41.548
What happens then, if
you're trying something,

00:40:41.548 --> 00:40:43.340
and it's not working,
and it's not working,

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:45.790
and it's not working.

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:49.110
So you have all these meetings
every week with people,

00:40:49.110 --> 00:40:55.670
and everybody will
agree what's happening.

00:40:55.670 --> 00:40:59.240
Hopefully, they will have some
other ideas about other paths

00:40:59.240 --> 00:41:01.270
to pursue.

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:04.980
Some people change
projects between the fall

00:41:04.980 --> 00:41:07.450
and the spring, but
that's pretty tricky,

00:41:07.450 --> 00:41:09.660
because you have to do
kind of all fall work.

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:16.160
And with a paper, you can
have negative results.

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:19.680
You can write up
everything you tried.

00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:25.680
I mean, that that's really
useful to other people working

00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:29.040
in the field, to
know what you tried.

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:32.730
Yeah.

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:37.170
Personally, I didn't have
any major road blocks.

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.050
But I know that some
people do, and I

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:42.960
think of you just communicate
with your supervisor,

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:44.920
it'll all work out in the end.

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:45.420
Yeah.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:49.170
I guess, now that I'm thinking
about it, one kind of downside

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:53.400
was in the fall, I think,
since you're ramping up

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:56.190
your research, within
the first two weeks,

00:41:56.190 --> 00:41:59.220
there's already things
due-- like an introduction.

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:06.210
And so it's totally OK to
not have your methods totally

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:07.020
fleshed out yet.

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:12.030
And just like having a starting
introduction and methods

00:42:12.030 --> 00:42:13.370
is always good.

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:15.453
And so you don't need to
freak out and be like,

00:42:15.453 --> 00:42:16.870
I haven't started
my research yet.

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:18.720
Like, what am I
supposed to be writing?

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:21.200
It's only two weeks in.

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:22.770
If you run into
trouble, you can also

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:25.720
go your TA and the
SuperUROP staff

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:29.780
and just let us know
and we can continue

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:35.102
to talk to the professor to
see if there's a way forward.

00:42:35.102 --> 00:42:40.882
We're all there to try to
provide some safety net.

00:42:40.882 --> 00:42:44.460
Yeah, that was another thing,
Is there's TAs and communication

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:46.410
instructors that you
work with, and so there's

00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:47.910
check-ins with that,
and they really

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:50.220
help with the writing
process and also

00:42:50.220 --> 00:42:51.910
putting together the poster.

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:55.470
And so I thought those
meetings were really helpful.

00:42:55.470 --> 00:42:58.950
Yeah, and so overall, I think
it was a really good experience,

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.200
and I highly recommend doing it.

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:04.680
It's really cool to just work
on your research over the course

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:07.260
of a whole year, and then
have the deliverables,

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:09.040
like the poster and
the paper, at the end

00:43:09.040 --> 00:43:11.040
to showcase everything
that you did.

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:13.740
And at the poster
presentations, you also

00:43:13.740 --> 00:43:15.578
get to talk to other
people in your field,

00:43:15.578 --> 00:43:17.370
and they can be interested
in your research

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:19.980
and then also tell you
what they've worked on.

00:43:19.980 --> 00:43:22.360
And it's just a really
interesting experience.

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:22.860
So yeah.

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:34.210
So thanks for coming.

00:43:34.210 --> 00:43:36.728
There's fliers with
dates in the back.

00:43:36.728 --> 00:43:38.020
There's a brochure in the back.

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:39.690
There's more pizza.

00:43:39.690 --> 00:43:42.390
Let us know if you
have any questions.

